Abstract = {Why does the public care more about some terrorist attacks than others?
   In recent years, there has been a wave of terrorist attacks carried out
   by similar terrorist organizations, but these attacks have produced
   disparate public responses. Existing research shows that terrorist
   attacks are more traumatic for people who live near terrorist targets,
   but this research cannot explain differences in public attitudes about
   attacks occurring in other countries. We argue that threat perceptions
   are shaped by the physical and personal proximity of terrorist attacks.
   The identities of the victims are rarely known. People impute the
   characteristics of victims based on the country where the attack
   occurred. These perceived identities determine the empathy people feel
   toward victims and affect perceptions of terrorist threats. People feel
   a greater sense of vulnerability when attacks occur near their borders.
   We test these arguments using a series of online experiments. We find
   that the location of the attack and the race and nationality of the
   victims drive threat perceptions.},
